WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.706 --> 00:00:02.999
- If you could sit next to Howard Schultz

00:00:02.999 --> 00:00:04.168
at a board meeting, which I do.

00:00:04.168 --> 00:00:05.168
I sit as close as I can.

00:00:05.168 --> 00:00:06.302
Breathe on me.

00:00:06.302 --> 00:00:08.360
Give me a little bit of it.

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:11.079
He said you don't go and
watch video from when you win.

00:00:11.079 --> 00:00:13.960
You watch video from when you lose.

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:15.307
It was one of those things where you think

00:00:15.307 --> 00:00:16.804
you're going to go in and
you're going to make a pitch

00:00:16.804 --> 00:00:18.366
and they're totally
going to shoot you down

00:00:18.366 --> 00:00:21.241
and then ultimately they say
yes and you're like holy shit.

00:00:21.241 --> 00:00:23.073
(group laughs)

00:00:23.073 --> 00:00:24.211
- Hello Believe Nation.

00:00:24.211 --> 00:00:25.542
I'm Evan Carmichael.

00:00:25.542 --> 00:00:27.071
My one word is believe and I believe that

00:00:27.071 --> 00:00:28.779
entrepreneurs are going to solve

00:00:28.779 --> 00:00:31.429
all of the world's major problems.

00:00:31.429 --> 00:00:33.189
So to help you on your journey
we're going to learn from

00:00:33.189 --> 00:00:35.639
Ariel Investments president Mellody Hobson

00:00:35.639 --> 00:00:38.693
and my take on her top
10 rules of success.

00:00:38.693 --> 00:00:40.877
Rule number 10 is my personal favorite

00:00:40.877 --> 00:00:42.943
and I'd love to know which
one you guys like the best

00:00:42.943 --> 00:00:46.057
and as always, as you're
listening, if something really

00:00:46.057 --> 00:00:47.912
resonates with you, there's
a message that really

00:00:47.912 --> 00:00:50.268
holds true to you, please
leave it down the comments

00:00:50.268 --> 00:00:52.502
below and put it in quotes
so that other people

00:00:52.502 --> 00:00:54.538
can be inspired and
when you write it down,

00:00:54.538 --> 00:00:58.180
it's much more likely to
stick with yourself as well.

00:00:58.180 --> 00:00:59.013
Enjoy.

00:01:02.086 --> 00:01:04.669
(upbeat music)

00:01:11.938 --> 00:01:13.564
- Be a fanatic.

00:01:13.564 --> 00:01:15.881
Warren Buffet supposedly interviews people

00:01:15.881 --> 00:01:17.514
and he says are you a fanatic?

00:01:17.514 --> 00:01:19.479
And when you meet the
fanatics, you know them

00:01:19.479 --> 00:01:21.708
and I don't use fanatics in a bad way.

00:01:21.708 --> 00:01:25.215
You know, it's this, the
people that I most admire

00:01:25.215 --> 00:01:29.844
have so loved their work
and so wanted a good outcome

00:01:29.844 --> 00:01:32.526
you understand why they're so successful

00:01:32.526 --> 00:01:36.735
and when I think about our
young leaders of color,

00:01:36.735 --> 00:01:39.097
I want to tell them just, you know,

00:01:39.097 --> 00:01:42.153
it's never going to get easier.

00:01:42.153 --> 00:01:44.853
You think it gets easier, it doesn't.

00:01:44.853 --> 00:01:46.486
It never gets easier.

00:01:46.486 --> 00:01:48.252
I'm still doing homework every weekend.

00:01:48.252 --> 00:01:50.521
I'm still, it never gets
easier and if anything,

00:01:50.521 --> 00:01:52.141
the stakes go up so much,

00:01:52.141 --> 00:01:54.560
you actually have to work even harder.

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:55.876
You have to be fanatical.

00:01:55.876 --> 00:01:57.465
You do not give up.

00:01:57.465 --> 00:01:58.772
You don't take it so personally.

00:01:58.772 --> 00:02:01.274
You figure out how you're going to get up.

00:02:01.274 --> 00:02:03.596
Do not let people talk
you out of your dreams.

00:02:03.596 --> 00:02:05.413
Do not let them tell you no

00:02:05.413 --> 00:02:09.249
and you own that you
have to convince them.

00:02:09.249 --> 00:02:12.777
I've spent time with
people who are world class.

00:02:12.777 --> 00:02:14.720
I know what world class looks like

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:16.845
and I have to bring that
back to my organization

00:02:16.845 --> 00:02:19.213
and bring that to life for them.

00:02:19.213 --> 00:02:23.471
But I can tell you with the
people that I spend time with,

00:02:23.471 --> 00:02:26.230
I am learning world class from them.

00:02:26.230 --> 00:02:27.492
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:02:27.492 --> 00:02:29.678
- And so therefore I have big dreams.

00:02:29.678 --> 00:02:31.635
If you could sit next to Howard
Schultz in a board meeting,

00:02:31.635 --> 00:02:32.913
which I do in every --

00:02:32.913 --> 00:02:34.103
I sit as close as I can.

00:02:34.103 --> 00:02:35.486
Breathe on me.

00:02:35.486 --> 00:02:38.172
Give me a little bit of it, you know?

00:02:38.172 --> 00:02:39.712
Look my way.

00:02:39.712 --> 00:02:41.970
No, I'im joking but I sit as, you know,

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:45.785
I'm watching how he
watches, how he's studying,

00:02:45.785 --> 00:02:47.732
how the presentation's being made,

00:02:47.732 --> 00:02:50.946
how he's interacting with
people and I am learning

00:02:50.946 --> 00:02:53.107
and I want to make sure
our people have that same

00:02:53.107 --> 00:02:54.709
level of energy around this.

00:02:54.709 --> 00:02:58.209
That's because that's what world class is.

00:02:59.372 --> 00:03:03.467
Do not make decisions
based on money, none.

00:03:03.467 --> 00:03:06.053
It's the worst way to make a decision,

00:03:06.053 --> 00:03:08.351
which I know is so hard to say.

00:03:08.351 --> 00:03:10.224
I mean I didn't grow up with any money

00:03:10.224 --> 00:03:11.950
and I know so many of us are,

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:14.651
our families have a lot at stake.

00:03:14.651 --> 00:03:17.855
They put a lot into us, made
a lot of sacrifices for us

00:03:17.855 --> 00:03:20.823
and you say you don't have the luxury

00:03:20.823 --> 00:03:24.817
of doing that, but I
actually think you do.

00:03:24.817 --> 00:03:27.984
You will be more successful
if you make decisions

00:03:27.984 --> 00:03:31.148
based upon what is
going to feed your soul,

00:03:31.148 --> 00:03:35.092
make you feel value, valued, validated,

00:03:35.092 --> 00:03:37.809
you know, have you
waking up in the morning

00:03:37.809 --> 00:03:39.652
and racing to work, whatever it might be,

00:03:39.652 --> 00:03:41.772
and I can tell you after
a short amount of time,

00:03:41.772 --> 00:03:44.267
money will not be enough.

00:03:44.267 --> 00:03:47.937
At the end of the day, a
bigger paycheck is another suit

00:03:47.937 --> 00:03:50.592
or more real estate, more square footage.

00:03:50.592 --> 00:03:53.050
That's what it comes down to.

00:03:53.050 --> 00:03:57.133
You end up with another
room and a fuller closet.

00:03:58.777 --> 00:04:02.431
And after a while, that's
not going to do it.

00:04:02.431 --> 00:04:07.217
I read this quote during an
obituary for Floyd Patterson

00:04:07.217 --> 00:04:11.821
the boxer and he said he only
learned from losing a fight.

00:04:11.821 --> 00:04:15.008
He said you don't go and
watch video from when you win.

00:04:15.008 --> 00:04:18.870
You watch video from when you
lose and it is the failures

00:04:18.870 --> 00:04:21.328
that build the character,
that make you smarter,

00:04:21.328 --> 00:04:22.911
stronger, tougher.

00:04:22.911 --> 00:04:24.654
I was told people were scarred but smarter

00:04:24.654 --> 00:04:27.671
and I think that is a really thing to be.

00:04:27.671 --> 00:04:30.343
You could build on all
that accumulated knowledge

00:04:30.343 --> 00:04:33.290
in a way that can serve you and
your clients or your company

00:04:33.290 --> 00:04:36.193
very very well, but there
have been, you know,

00:04:36.193 --> 00:04:40.026
numerous moments of great
pause and reflection

00:04:41.372 --> 00:04:44.250
where I've sat and say,
and I say to our team

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:47.311
and I say to myself, what is the universe

00:04:47.311 --> 00:04:48.681
trying to teach me?

00:04:48.681 --> 00:04:50.429
That idea of I'm not
looking out five years,

00:04:50.429 --> 00:04:52.788
I'm looking out long-term,
I'm not thinking about

00:04:52.788 --> 00:04:55.248
there's another job for me somewhere else.

00:04:55.248 --> 00:04:57.538
I'm thinking about this job right now.

00:04:57.538 --> 00:04:59.892
Those are the kinds of
things that, you know,

00:04:59.892 --> 00:05:02.879
I think have been able
to lead to my success.

00:05:02.879 --> 00:05:05.650
When I started working
at Ariel, our founder

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:07.503
John Rogers said something to me

00:05:07.503 --> 00:05:09.449
that I have never forgotten.

00:05:09.449 --> 00:05:13.532
On my very first day, he
took me to TGI Friday's,

00:05:14.491 --> 00:05:17.408
big spender, and he said every game

00:05:20.029 --> 00:05:23.770
in life is won with
patience, every single one.

00:05:23.770 --> 00:05:25.644
And he said I don't care if it's dating,

00:05:25.644 --> 00:05:28.526
to you can't win the Indianapolis 500

00:05:28.526 --> 00:05:31.583
in the first lap to a basketball game.

00:05:31.583 --> 00:05:35.220
If you look at all
sports, just a very simple

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:37.811
just one sort of portion of the world,

00:05:37.811 --> 00:05:41.089
patience is how you win
and that has been something

00:05:41.089 --> 00:05:44.256
that I intuitively got at that moment.

00:05:45.999 --> 00:05:47.868
I think when I think
about what I'd like to be

00:05:47.868 --> 00:05:50.841
remembered for, I would
like to be thought of

00:05:50.841 --> 00:05:55.000
having been very kind,
who did right by society.

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:58.455
I actually learned that
probably in my most

00:05:58.455 --> 00:06:02.292
formative years from my
business partner John Rogers.

00:06:02.292 --> 00:06:04.105
I actually just read a graduation speech

00:06:04.105 --> 00:06:06.030
that someone gave at
one of the universities

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:09.613
about kindness and I
thought that he really

00:06:12.700 --> 00:06:15.116
spoke to everything that
John started to tell me

00:06:15.116 --> 00:06:18.529
when I was 22, that you could
always take the kind path

00:06:18.529 --> 00:06:22.389
and you could win with
kindness and I try to think

00:06:22.389 --> 00:06:24.316
a lot about that and
it doesn't always work

00:06:24.316 --> 00:06:27.662
and sometimes I know I'm
not as kind as I want to be,

00:06:27.662 --> 00:06:30.738
but it's in the back of my mind a lot.

00:06:30.738 --> 00:06:33.732
I sit a lot of rooms with people who wait,

00:06:33.732 --> 00:06:37.147
wait for someone to say
something or my favorite line is,

00:06:37.147 --> 00:06:38.603
I give this all the time.

00:06:38.603 --> 00:06:40.850
At Princeton, we had a
professor and if you were

00:06:40.850 --> 00:06:43.155
in the preset group with
him and someone spoke

00:06:43.155 --> 00:06:45.551
and you said I want to
reiterate what Sally said,

00:06:45.551 --> 00:06:47.989
he would say you mean iterate again,

00:06:47.989 --> 00:06:51.489
which meant you have no original thoughts.

00:06:52.824 --> 00:06:55.356
And so these are the kinds
of things I think about:

00:06:55.356 --> 00:06:58.578
have an original idea,
be willing to speak it

00:06:58.578 --> 00:07:00.199
because it will get you noticed.

00:07:00.199 --> 00:07:04.331
I love to swim so much that as
an adult I swim with a coach.

00:07:04.331 --> 00:07:07.048
And one day my coach had me do a drill

00:07:07.048 --> 00:07:09.892
where I had to swim to
one end of a 25 meter pool

00:07:09.892 --> 00:07:13.852
without taking a breath and
every single time I failed

00:07:13.852 --> 00:07:16.926
I have to start over and I failed a lot.

00:07:16.926 --> 00:07:19.243
By the end I got it, but
I got out of the pool,

00:07:19.243 --> 00:07:21.520
I was exasperated and tired and annoyed.

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:24.919
And I said why are we doing
breath holding exercises?

00:07:24.919 --> 00:07:27.260
And my coach looked at
me and he said Mellody,

00:07:27.260 --> 00:07:29.995
that was not a breath holding exercise.

00:07:29.995 --> 00:07:34.151
That drill was to make you
comfortable being uncomfortable

00:07:34.151 --> 00:07:37.712
because that's how most
of us spend our days.

00:07:37.712 --> 00:07:40.127
If we could learn to
deal with our discomfort

00:07:40.127 --> 00:07:44.266
and just relax into it,
we'll have a better life.

00:07:44.266 --> 00:07:46.282
I think there was a very
specific risk that I took

00:07:46.282 --> 00:07:48.026
very early on at Ariel that was

00:07:48.026 --> 00:07:50.581
unique and unprecedented at the time.

00:07:50.581 --> 00:07:52.001
I was about 24 years old.

00:07:52.001 --> 00:07:54.544
I had been there for a
couple years and we had these

00:07:54.544 --> 00:07:57.061
public mutual funds
that were in partnership

00:07:57.061 --> 00:07:59.648
with another company and I went to

00:07:59.648 --> 00:08:02.943
our Board of Directors and
said we need a divorce.

00:08:02.943 --> 00:08:04.889
It had never happened
before and we pulled apart

00:08:04.889 --> 00:08:09.389
two operating mutual funds
from an existing mutual fund

00:08:09.389 --> 00:08:13.241
family, $400 million in
assets, paid $400 million

00:08:13.241 --> 00:08:15.659
for the right to do it,
it was the whole nest egg

00:08:15.659 --> 00:08:17.353
of the company and that was

00:08:17.353 --> 00:08:19.620
a very, very, very bold call at the time.

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:21.223
It was one of those things
where you think you're

00:08:21.223 --> 00:08:22.752
going to go in, you're going
to make a pitch and they're

00:08:22.752 --> 00:08:24.641
totally going to shoot you
down and then ultimately

00:08:24.641 --> 00:08:27.570
they say yes and you're like holy shit.

00:08:27.570 --> 00:08:29.340
(group laughs)

00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:30.956
They're not supposed
to be listening to me.

00:08:30.956 --> 00:08:33.367
Don't they know I don't
know what I'm doing?

00:08:33.367 --> 00:08:36.998
But it ended up like
marrying me to the company.

00:08:36.998 --> 00:08:38.005
- Right.

00:08:38.005 --> 00:08:39.506
- It's where Ariel became mine.

00:08:39.506 --> 00:08:41.754
Now on a day to day basis,
what I'm now pushing

00:08:41.754 --> 00:08:43.538
very hard and Catherine Berardi is with me

00:08:43.538 --> 00:08:48.524
who is my teammate at Ariel
and a spectacular superstar

00:08:48.524 --> 00:08:51.654
and she's heard me now say
this story like 100 times

00:08:51.654 --> 00:08:54.516
in four months, but my new
thing that I'm really pushing

00:08:54.516 --> 00:08:57.248
very, very hard especially
with women and my friend

00:08:57.248 --> 00:08:59.067
Sheryl Sandberg is also
doing the same thing

00:08:59.067 --> 00:09:01.320
in her own magnificent way,

00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:03.461
I can't even compare myself
because she has a book

00:09:03.461 --> 00:09:06.513
that's coming out on this
idea, but I feel like

00:09:06.513 --> 00:09:10.420
as women, the risk is
us speaking our truth

00:09:10.420 --> 00:09:14.005
and the story that I'm now been telling

00:09:14.005 --> 00:09:16.778
is this story that I read
about a woman in a magazine

00:09:16.778 --> 00:09:19.796
who said every single
day, every single day

00:09:19.796 --> 00:09:22.358
her daughter walked out
of the house starting

00:09:22.358 --> 00:09:24.696
when she was a very small child, she said

00:09:24.696 --> 00:09:26.495
the exact same thing
to her every single day

00:09:26.495 --> 00:09:27.497
through college.

00:09:27.497 --> 00:09:28.330
Be brave.

00:09:29.333 --> 00:09:30.869
Every single day.

00:09:30.869 --> 00:09:32.340
She said she wrote her letters in college

00:09:32.340 --> 00:09:33.960
and she always signed them be brave

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:36.101
and that now just speaks
to me because I'm in so

00:09:36.101 --> 00:09:40.711
many rooms where I feel like
the courage is not always there

00:09:40.711 --> 00:09:43.169
and I don't know what the fear is.

00:09:43.169 --> 00:09:45.605
Maybe it's the fear of
not saying the right thing

00:09:45.605 --> 00:09:47.962
or not fighting in but
I'd rather the person

00:09:47.962 --> 00:09:50.805
totally, you know, make a giant mistake

00:09:50.805 --> 00:09:53.759
than not say anything
at all and I just find

00:09:53.759 --> 00:09:55.956
with women, sometimes we're really sort of

00:09:55.956 --> 00:09:58.922
waiting to add our voice
and I don't mean to say this

00:09:58.922 --> 00:10:00.775
in any way that diminishes us

00:10:00.775 --> 00:10:02.556
because we have the good ideas,

00:10:02.556 --> 00:10:06.723
but you know, when I was at
Princeton in our presets,

00:10:07.668 --> 00:10:09.991
our small groups when
we weren't in lecture,

00:10:09.991 --> 00:10:13.406
they would say something to
you that if you were piling on

00:10:13.406 --> 00:10:15.568
with what someone else said
and you say I'd like to

00:10:15.568 --> 00:10:17.724
reiterate what this
person said, they'd say

00:10:17.724 --> 00:10:20.896
oh you mean iterate again
and that was their way

00:10:20.896 --> 00:10:23.883
of making you see like that
is not an original thought.

00:10:23.883 --> 00:10:24.883
- Right.

00:10:24.883 --> 00:10:26.349
- And I feel like a lot
of the women in the room

00:10:26.349 --> 00:10:28.835
have their original
thought, in some situations,

00:10:28.835 --> 00:10:32.359
and are waiting to be
validated and I'm just like

00:10:32.359 --> 00:10:36.140
go for it, go for broke, pound
the tables, speak your truth

00:10:36.140 --> 00:10:39.551
and I now notice it in
ways even in my own firm

00:10:39.551 --> 00:10:41.951
where I give people real life examples.

00:10:41.951 --> 00:10:44.078
We had a board meeting
recently at our firm

00:10:44.078 --> 00:10:46.971
and one of my colleagues
who's a superstar woman

00:10:46.971 --> 00:10:51.221
was presenting and she was
only looking at our CEO

00:10:51.221 --> 00:10:54.990
when she was talking and I was like,

00:10:54.990 --> 00:10:57.285
don't ever do that again.

00:10:57.285 --> 00:10:59.966
It was like she was
looking for his validation

00:10:59.966 --> 00:11:01.948
and all the other people around the table,

00:11:01.948 --> 00:11:04.952
it was like they didn't matter.

00:11:04.952 --> 00:11:09.000
And I was like, it just made
you look small and you're not.

00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:12.850
And that was like a simple
example of that being brave.

00:11:12.850 --> 00:11:14.885
What about her needed to lock on on him

00:11:14.885 --> 00:11:17.475
and have him nod and you
know, have her keep going

00:11:17.475 --> 00:11:20.437
versus just owning the
room with her presence.

00:11:20.437 --> 00:11:22.369
Stand up for what you believe in.

00:11:22.369 --> 00:11:23.786
Do not back down.

00:11:25.384 --> 00:11:26.750
And that doesn't mean you have to do it

00:11:26.750 --> 00:11:30.917
with a fist in the air or
you know with aggression.

00:11:32.290 --> 00:11:35.373
You can do it with grace and I cannot

00:11:36.225 --> 00:11:38.489
be talked out of what I believe.

00:11:38.489 --> 00:11:41.389
Now that doesn't mean, like
I mean in my core values.

00:11:41.389 --> 00:11:45.345
You cannot convince me that
racism will ever be a path

00:11:45.345 --> 00:11:46.587
to something good.

00:11:46.587 --> 00:11:48.780
You cannot convince me
that excluding people

00:11:48.780 --> 00:11:50.955
ever leads to a better outcome.

00:11:50.955 --> 00:11:52.780
You cannot convince me of that.

00:11:52.780 --> 00:11:54.841
I will not be moved.

00:11:54.841 --> 00:11:56.605
I will not compromise that point of view.

00:11:56.605 --> 00:11:59.060
And as a result of that,
I'm going to do everything

00:11:59.060 --> 00:12:02.407
I can to keep pressing those
perspectives and I think

00:12:02.407 --> 00:12:05.087
if any issue I have with
some of the millennials

00:12:05.087 --> 00:12:07.348
that I've had the opportunity
to work up close with,

00:12:07.348 --> 00:12:08.660
don't be compliant.

00:12:08.660 --> 00:12:11.529
You know, just, you are taught sometimes

00:12:11.529 --> 00:12:14.510
winning is the path of least resistance

00:12:14.510 --> 00:12:17.043
and I think this is now
more than ever your voice

00:12:17.043 --> 00:12:20.338
and you know, being willing
to put yourself in harm's way.

00:12:20.338 --> 00:12:21.892
It's like the question of losing a job.

00:12:21.892 --> 00:12:23.955
I know it sounds like I'm being flip.

00:12:23.955 --> 00:12:25.487
I have often asked
myself do I belong here,

00:12:25.487 --> 00:12:28.225
should I walk out, nothing is worth this.

00:12:28.225 --> 00:12:31.250
And you know, I think that you know,

00:12:31.250 --> 00:12:33.512
just really pushing
yourself to understand that.

00:12:33.512 --> 00:12:35.077
Sometimes just certain
things, you're just going

00:12:35.077 --> 00:12:37.031
to have to walk away if
you really, really, really

00:12:37.031 --> 00:12:39.914
believe that you've got
to stand up for something.

00:12:39.914 --> 00:12:43.552
So you know, I went to this
program at The Aspen Institute,

00:12:43.552 --> 00:12:45.744
the Henry Crown Fellows
program and we were in these

00:12:45.744 --> 00:12:48.347
small leadership groups,
20 people and Skip Battle,

00:12:48.347 --> 00:12:51.382
who's this amazing guy and
known in Silicon Valley

00:12:51.382 --> 00:12:52.611
was my moderator.

00:12:52.611 --> 00:12:55.109
I have so much respect
and admiration for him.

00:12:55.109 --> 00:12:56.618
And one day we were in the
room and he said to me,

00:12:56.618 --> 00:12:58.403
Mellody I know you will do good.

00:12:58.403 --> 00:12:59.680
I was like 30 days old.

00:12:59.680 --> 00:13:02.219
He goes, but will you stand in harm's way?

00:13:02.219 --> 00:13:04.200
Will you put yourself in harm's way?

00:13:04.200 --> 00:13:06.104
He said you could go
through your whole life

00:13:06.104 --> 00:13:09.753
just doing the easy path and
feeling good about yourself

00:13:09.753 --> 00:13:11.279
and patting yourself on your back,

00:13:11.279 --> 00:13:14.201
but will you allow yourself to be hurt?

00:13:14.201 --> 00:13:16.898
Will you allow yourself to
have a tremendous setback

00:13:16.898 --> 00:13:19.691
because you stood up
for what you believed?

00:13:19.691 --> 00:13:21.526
And that forced me to really think about

00:13:21.526 --> 00:13:25.342
the path of least resistance
and saying versus believing

00:13:25.342 --> 00:13:28.349
that I will allow myself to be harmed.

00:13:28.349 --> 00:13:30.915
And then I started to
think about people like

00:13:30.915 --> 00:13:34.235
John Lewis standing on
the Edmund Pettus Bridge

00:13:34.235 --> 00:13:35.692
and I started to think, I literally

00:13:35.692 --> 00:13:37.457
cannot say this without welling up,

00:13:37.457 --> 00:13:39.972
saying could I have that kind of courage?

00:13:39.972 --> 00:13:42.495
And I think this is the moment
we're standing in right now.

00:13:42.495 --> 00:13:44.586
We have to have that John Lewis courage,

00:13:44.586 --> 00:13:47.789
that Martin Luther King
courage, that dogs turned on

00:13:47.789 --> 00:13:50.793
teenagers, fire hoses
turned on people courage

00:13:50.793 --> 00:13:53.031
'cause I think that if
we were standing there,

00:13:53.031 --> 00:13:55.436
if I was standing there,
I think I would run.

00:13:55.436 --> 00:13:57.853
Now I'm telling myself stand.

00:14:00.801 --> 00:14:02.116
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:14:02.116 --> 00:14:05.013
I made this video because
Sarah Green asked me to.

00:14:05.013 --> 00:14:06.234
If there's someone
you'd like me to profile

00:14:06.234 --> 00:14:09.170
in the next top 10, check out
the link in the description

00:14:09.170 --> 00:14:11.416
and go and cast your vote.

00:14:11.416 --> 00:14:13.147
I'd also like to know
what was your favorite

00:14:13.147 --> 00:14:15.485
message from this video, what
did you learn from this video

00:14:15.485 --> 00:14:18.056
that you're going to
immediately apply somehow

00:14:18.056 --> 00:14:19.713
in your life or in your business?

00:14:19.713 --> 00:14:21.332
Please leave it down
in the comments below.

00:14:21.332 --> 00:14:22.612
I'm really curious to find out.

00:14:22.612 --> 00:14:24.555
I also want to give a quick
shout out to the Essetino

00:14:24.555 --> 00:14:26.082
Artists YouTube channel.

00:14:26.082 --> 00:14:27.639
Thank you guys so much for
picking up a copy of my book

00:14:27.639 --> 00:14:30.128
Your One Word and having me on your show

00:14:30.128 --> 00:14:32.577
in an interview to talk about it.

00:14:32.577 --> 00:14:34.067
I really, really, really
appreciate the support

00:14:34.067 --> 00:14:36.571
and I'm so glad that you enjoyed the book.

00:14:36.571 --> 00:14:39.253
- We're interviewing
author of Your One Word,

00:14:39.253 --> 00:14:43.302
Evan Carmichael live
on our YouTube channel.

00:14:43.302 --> 00:14:44.544
- So thank you guys again for watching.

00:14:44.544 --> 00:14:46.056
I believe in you.

00:14:46.056 --> 00:14:47.797
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:14:47.797 --> 00:14:49.943
and whatever your one word is.

00:14:49.943 --> 00:14:52.360
Much love, I'll see you soon.

00:14:58.940 --> 00:15:01.010
- If you follow your passion,
you're going to be successful

00:15:01.010 --> 00:15:03.614
no matter what because
one, you'll be happy,

00:15:03.614 --> 00:15:06.714
and I think happiness is really important.

00:15:06.714 --> 00:15:08.696
You can't do anything
well if you're not happy.

00:15:08.696 --> 00:15:10.639
You can't be a good parent, good spouse,

00:15:10.639 --> 00:15:14.869
girlfriend, boyfriend, you
can't be a good co-worker.

00:15:14.869 --> 00:15:17.041
Happiness is important
and that doesn't mean

00:15:17.041 --> 00:15:21.438
euphoria everyday, but
a basic sense of peace

00:15:21.438 --> 00:15:24.130
and for some of us, especially
Type A and especially

00:15:24.130 --> 00:15:27.211
hard on ourselves, we
don't give each other,

00:15:27.211 --> 00:15:30.428
give ourselves a break and
so the pursuit of happiness

00:15:30.428 --> 00:15:33.497
as we have it written
obviously in our Declaration

00:15:33.497 --> 00:15:37.787
of Independence is something
that is challenging.

00:15:37.787 --> 00:15:40.398
So pursuing happiness
I do think is important

00:15:40.398 --> 00:15:43.422
as pursuit of anything else.

00:15:43.422 --> 00:15:47.589
Observe your environment,
at work, at school, at home.

00:15:48.659 --> 00:15:50.946
I'm asking you to look
at the people around you.

00:15:50.946 --> 00:15:54.542
Purposefully and
intentionally invite people

00:15:54.542 --> 00:15:56.971
into your life who don't look like you,

00:15:56.971 --> 00:15:59.440
don't think like you, don't act like you,

00:15:59.440 --> 00:16:01.411
don't come from where you come from

00:16:01.411 --> 00:16:03.306
and you might find that
they will challenge

00:16:03.306 --> 00:16:06.042
your assumptions and make
you grow as a person.

00:16:06.042 --> 00:16:09.464
You might get powerful new
insights from these individuals.

00:16:09.464 --> 00:16:12.806
Some of our best ideas
also come from pushing

00:16:12.806 --> 00:16:16.192
something that other
people are afraid to do.

00:16:16.192 --> 00:16:19.649
So we've innovated in
areas that cause people

00:16:19.649 --> 00:16:21.923
some trepidation and I think that's just

00:16:21.923 --> 00:16:24.470
the part of us that is a little irreverent

00:16:24.470 --> 00:16:28.174
and the part of us that's willing
to be very very different.

00:16:28.174 --> 00:16:30.432
So it's everything from
having a turtle as a logo

00:16:30.432 --> 00:16:33.889
when Merryl has a scary
bull and Drefyus has a lion.

00:16:33.889 --> 00:16:35.615
And we have this turtle
holding a loving cup

00:16:35.615 --> 00:16:38.288
that was a drawing and you
know looks very friendly.

00:16:38.288 --> 00:16:39.733
That was very different.

00:16:39.733 --> 00:16:41.734
So we had this turtle, it
was John's original logo

00:16:41.734 --> 00:16:45.316
for Ariel and then we just blew it out.

00:16:45.316 --> 00:16:48.053
I mean all of our materials
look like storybooks

00:16:48.053 --> 00:16:50.297
and our brochure was like a storybook

00:16:50.297 --> 00:16:51.687
and people would get them and say

00:16:51.687 --> 00:16:53.521
I took this home.

00:16:53.521 --> 00:16:56.380
And then you knew you
had something special.

